00:05:43.181 --> 00:06:07.026
And I have here some quotes about that. He says, curiously enough, I have critics who think that I, of all people, want to replace the living psyche by intellectual concepts. I do not understand how they have managed to overlook the fact that my concepts are based on empirical findings, are nothing but names for certain areas of experience.

00:06:08.469 --> 00:06:33.443
So this is a stance that Jung has very, very often. He says, he starts with the phenomenon first. He sees something, he notices something, and he says, okay, this cannot really be denied away. And the theory has to follow that. Meaning he tries to come up with the ideas based on the findings and experiences that he had. But he was also pretty humble about that.

00:06:34.328 --> 00:06:50.989
Jung makes no absolute claim on the validity of his theories and ideas. He says that is how he sees the world. This is the stuff that he notices and how he explains how different things relate to each other. And how they likely work.

