But we can talk about what is like some tenets, some really paradigms that we need for scientific inquiry. And the first thing is it has to be testable. Meaning a lot of science is done in a way you try to find something out and you're trying to disprove it and you're trying to cut away basically everything that you can falsify.

00:18:03.388 --> 00:18:26.404
And what you cannot falsify and is left at the end and you can find no way around it, this is then true. So you don't necessarily prove that it's true. You disprove that it can be Disproven. So for that, things has to be very precise and they have to be precise statements.

00:18:26.831 --> 00:18:44.847
And Jung makes a lot of precise statements, but he also does some vague statements. So what we will be focusing at today will be really testable as they were stated by Jung. And then the question is, okay, how do you test? It can be anecdotes, case reports, or similar things.

00:18:44.977 --> 00:19:02.625
When Jung says, oh, he has a single patient who had this experience and he saw this thing, this is then not really a scientific study. So you try to make larger groups and you try to compare groups. And kind of a gold standard is when you have randomized controlled trials.

00:19:02.881 --> 00:19:26.826
This is how they test medication, for example, where you say you take a group that is similar and you split it into 2 groups. And you treat one group one way and one group the other way. And any difference in them, you can see, okay, it has to be in the difference what you do. For example, one group gets a placebo, the other group gets a new medication.

00:19:27.259 --> 00:19:50.255
When the group with the new medication improves, it has to be, or is very likely, because of that medication. This is why you have to run huge groups and you have to done it multiple times. So when you have there these controlled experiments, you do that and try to do that ideally again and again.

00:19:50.867 --> 00:20:09.306
And then you have something called meta-analysis when people are looking at multiple, multiple, multiple studies to verify how well they have worked, how scientific they are, how factors they took into consideration, and so on. This is the highest form of science that is possible and available for us now.

